schema: claim-evidence
object:
  title: Most rejected DAO proposals fail due to clarity/scoping, not idea quality
  type: claim-evidence
  claim: >-
    "Most rejected proposals fail not because the idea is bad but because the
    proposal is unclear, poorly scoped, or doesn't address community
    concerns." (proposal-writing.md, "Why Proposals Fail")
  evidence: >-
    No specific study, dataset, or named DAO case is cited. Article
    frontmatter lists a single generic source: "Community governance" (no
    further citation detail).
  source_lineage: "src/content/docs/proposal-writing.md (frontmatter sources field: Community governance)"
  interpretation: >-
    Used as the article's foundational justification for the entire
    proposal-anatomy and pre-proposal-process guidance that follows —
    reframes proposal failure as a communication/process problem rather than
    a merit problem.
  uncertainty: >-
    Presented as a general truth with no supporting data, named example, or
    citation beyond a vague "Community governance" source tag. Plausible based
    on common DAO-governance folk wisdom, but unverified in this article and
    should be treated as an assertion pending evidence.
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/proposal-writing.md
    transformation: quoted
    authorship: ai-assisted

# assessed-but-excluded: the article is short (~1 page) and generic (no named
# DAOs, tools, or specific case studies) — the "Common Mistakes" and "Moving
# Forward" sections were folded into the concept-lineage/encyclopedia-entry
# summaries rather than split into further objects, since they add no new
# citable claims or distinct entities beyond the proposal-anatomy template
# already captured.
